message.reasoning_content:
We need to examine the errors from acceptance test failures and TypeScript errors as indicated in static validation. The acceptance test failures include:

- test failures in various features like verification after export etc.
- TypeScript errors about imports: ledgerVerification.ts imports wrong members from @noble/hashes/sha2.js: it tries to import sha256, utf8ToBytes, bytesToHex, but these aren't exported from that module (sha256 is exported from @noble/hashes/sha2, but utf8ToBytes, bytesToHex are exported from @noble/hashes/utils). In ledgerVerification.ts import statement is wrong.

- ReceiptHistoryScreen.tsx: component expects 'icon' property missing (maybe from the EmptyState component?). Actually error: 'Property 'icon' is missing in type '{ title: string; body: string; actionLabel: string; onAction: () => void; }' but required in type 'Props'. That is in ReceiptHistoryScreen or maybe a component used there: EmptyState component expects icon field. The code in ReceiptHistoryScreen uses <EmptyState title=... body=... actionLabel=... onAction=...>. Probably need to include icon field.

- Many acceptance test failures indicate that verifyExportCode isn't returning correct 'laterChanges' boolean. Also verifyExportCode's behavior might be wrong. Also verification after export being not detected.

- verifyExportCode expects ledger entry format may be wrong.

- The export.created ledger entry code: In appendEntryForExport, they create data: { format, code, entries: entriesCount, headHash: headHashPrev }. They set prevHash to previous ledger's hash, but they ask to record entries and headHash. The requirement: "append entry for export" kind "export.created" with fields: format, code, entries, headHash (the hash of the latest ledger entry). In spec, the export.created entry's data has fields format, code, entries, headHash (points to headHash BEFORE the entry, i.e., at time of export). The current code uses headHashPrev, which is the previous headHash (i.e., the hash of previous entry). That seems correct. However some code expects entry.data.headHash to be headHash of latest ledger entry (maybe after export, the entry itself is latest). Need to confirm test expectations.

In acceptance test m11 (line 75-81 for PDF export verification), they check:

```
const exportEntry = ledger.find(e => e.kind === 'export.created' && e.data?.format === 'pdf');
expect(exportEntry).toBeDefined();
const data = exportEntry?.data;
expect(data?.format).toBe('pdf');
expect(data?.code).toMatch(/^[0-9a-f]{12}$/);
expect(data?.entries).toBe(ledger.length);
expect(data?.headHash).toBe(ledger[ledger.length - 1].hash);
```

Here they expect export data entries count to be equal to the length of ledger (the final ledger length after adding the export entry). And export entry .data.headHash equal to ledger[ledger.length - 1].hash (the latest ledger entry). But .data.headHash is currently set to headHashPrev (the previous head hash before adding export entry). So after adding export entry, ledger[ledger.length - 1] is the export entry, its hash is new hash. The previous entry had hash = headHashPrev. In test they expect data.headHash equal to ledger[ledger.length - 1].hash, which is the hash of the export entry itself? That seems contradictory. Actually spec says headHash is hash of latest ledger entry at export time; which is the entry before the export entry, which is headHashPrev. But test expects data.headHash to be ledger[ledger.length - 1].hash (the export entry). So test expects that export entry data includes headHash of its own hash? Or maybe the test expects headHash to be after export, not before. Let's read spec: R28: After each CSV or PDF export, src/lib/ledger.ts must append a ledger entry of kind "export.created" with fields: format, code, entries (the ledger entry count at export time), and headHash (the hash of the latest ledger entry). This 'latest' likely refers to the entry just before the export entry? Or after? It could be ambiguous. But test expects after entry includes its own hash? Actually ledger length includes the export entry; they compare entries count to ledger.length (including export entry). They compare headHash to ledger[ledger.length - 1].hash which is the export entry itself. So they think entries count should be count after adding export entry (i.e., includes the export entry). And headHash is the headHash after adding export entry (the export entry's hash). This seems logically contradictory but test is clear.

Thus we need to adjust appendEntryForExport to set data.headHash = entry.hash (the new entry's hash) and entriesCount = ledger.length (including new entry). That is likely why test fails.

Also need to adjust verification code in verifyExportCode to detect later changes.

Also acceptance test for verification after export: they perform a receipt correction after generating export (CSV) and then verifyExportCode (code). They expect laterChanges = true. Our verifyExportCode probably does not compute laterChanges.

We need to view src/lib/exportVerification.ts.

